      "id": 1,
      "text": "When Benton County split from Yakima and Klickitat in 1905, Prosser was selected as its new county seat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosser%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was founded by Colonel William Farrand Prosser, who surveyed the area in 1879 and claimed a homestead in 1882.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosser%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Prosser’s first irrigation canal, completed in 1893 by the Prosser Falls Land and Irrigation Company, sparked agricultural growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosser%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Northern Pacific Railroad laid tracks through Prosser two years after the town’s founding, linking it to regional trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosser%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Prosser hosts the historic Princess Theatre, a 1920s venue that still stages live performances and movies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
